  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-06T08:17:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "799a68ee-4816-4dc9-86e8-01a49e393a7d",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 060817\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n117 AM PDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Our next system brings cooler temperatures, gusty winds and\n  precipitation chances this weekend into next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nBroad ridging remains in place through the work week, keeping \ntemperatures well above normal (6-12 degrees) for early October. The \nheat will be most pronounced in Death Valley National Park, where \nhighs near 110 and lows in the 80s yield major to extreme HeatRisk. \nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Friday evening for \nelevations below 2000' in the park. Elsewhere, minor to moderate \nHeatRisk is anticipated with the above normal temperatures. There \nare low chances (20%) for isolated showers and a few rumbles of \nthunder in Lincoln County this afternoon, but otherwise dry \nconditions persist.\n\nThe forecast becomes rather complicated over the weekend and into \nearly next week as multiple features interact with each other and \ninfluence weather across the western US. The three features of note \nare a shortwave coming out of western Canada, a cutoff low off the \nCA coast, and Hurricane Rachel. Confidence is pretty high in \nwidespread gusty winds and cooler temperatures beginning Saturday. \nThe main uncertainty remains on the precipitation side. Latest \nguidance has trended wetter, particularly the eastern half of the \nCWA. The NBM and raw ensemble guidance have 20-50% chances of 0.50\"+ \ngenerally over the Spring Mountains and points east (chances \nincreasing with eastward extent) during the Sunday-Monday timeframe. \nOdds of 1\"+ are 10-30% over the same area and time. Don't want to \nget too far ahead of ourselves, as predicting the interaction of the \n3 features at this range is a tall task and small changes will make \na big difference in precipitation potential. However, this system \nand forecast trends will certainly be something to watch, so stay \ntuned.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds following typical diurnal directional patterns will continue \nthrough the forecast period. At times, winds will vary in direction \ndue to the light speeds, but will favor the east in the afternoon \nand the southwest overnight. Can't rule out a gust over 15 kts (20 \npercent) from the east with the heating of the day. No operationally \nsignificant cloud cover expected through the TAF period. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical diurnal directional patterns will continue through the \nforecast period. Expect occasional gusts to 15 kts with the heating \nof the day at Las Vegas Valley TAF sites from the east... at KBIH \nfrom the south-southeast... and at KDAG from the east. No \noperationally significant cloud cover through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     100(2024)   99(1978)*   98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        94(2024)    94(1965)*   95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       111(2024)   107(2024)   107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       105(2024)   102(2024)   101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  110(2024)*  109(2024)*  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     74(2024)*   75(2024)*   76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        52(2018)    57(1943)    51(1960)    61(2025)\nNeedles       77(2023)*   76(1947)*   76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       75(2024)    72(2024)*   69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       67(1949)*   69(1947)*   67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   66(1990)*   65(1980)*   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  83(1943)*   80(1943)*   85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Soulat\nCLIMATE...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
